# TODO - Canonical asset contract

## Objective

Define the shared asset format and manifest consumed by runtimes.

## Acceptance

- Record the selected source revision and SHA-256 at onboarding.
- Keep source-platform knowledge out of gameplay and runtime crates.
- Use synthetic fixtures for integrity and compatibility tests.

## Evidence

The shared `games-assets` crate defines versioned JSON manifests, canonical
logical identifiers, SHA-256 payloads, explicit per-entry and aggregate
budgets, RGBA8 images and interleaved signed 16-bit PCM audio. Synthetic
tests cover deterministic ordering, format size validation, tampering,
duplicate keys, unsupported versions and malformed metadata.
The first original archive is pinned in the Simpsons task. Synthetic
importer conversion exercises canonical descriptors and manifests. Runtime
consumption and target compatibility still require evidence.

The canonical format also defines indexed tiles with an explicit tile
count, width and height, and one byte per palette index. The selected
Simpsons converter emits two validated indexed-tile assets. These data
are not yet rendered, and palette interpretation remains title-specific.

The native bundle reader pins the exact canonical manifest bytes by
SHA-256, validates metadata limits and reads only declared logical keys
from descriptor-relative storage. Every payload is size- and hash-checked
on demand. Synthetic tests reject changed content, oversized files,
noncanonical manifests, unsafe links, special files and writable-by-others
storage. The writer and reader share storage naming and trust checks.
Fresh validation passed in run 1795578729856852353-3030117 (147 tests).
A trusted caller must supply the expected manifest digest; this is not
an authenticity or same-user sandbox guarantee. Android device and browser
consumption remain unverified.
